<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml" xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:CafePosApp.ViewModels" xmlns:models="clr-namespace:CafePos.Core.Models;assembly=CafePos.Core" x:Class="CafePosApp.Views.MenuPage" x:DataType="vm:MenuViewModel" Title="Меню">
    <ContentPage.Resources>
        <!-- One chip template for the whole strip, including the synthetic "Все" chip. It now takes
             its size and shape from the shared FilterChip style — the hand-rolled 36dp chip was under
             the 44dp touch minimum — while keeping the per-category pastel fill and the selected
             tint the menu page has always used for its visual coding. -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="CategoryChipTemplate" x:DataType="vm:CategoryMenuItemViewModel">
            <Border Style="{StaticResource FilterChip}"
                    HeightRequest="44"
                    BackgroundColor="{Binding ChipBackground}">
                <Label Text="{Binding Name}"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"
                       LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"
                       TextColor="{Binding ChipTextColor}"
                       FontSize="13"
                       FontAttributes="Bold"
                       VerticalTextAlignment="Center" />
                <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.SelectCategoryCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            </Border>
        </DataTemplate>
    </ContentPage.Resources>

    <!-- ═══ ROOT ═══ -->
    <!-- Three rows: the chip strip, the product list, the cart.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         WHY THERE IS NO SEARCH ON THIS PAGE
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The owner's decision: «Удали поиск вообще. У меня в приложении не так много позиций, чтобы
         не найти их просто скроллом.» The catalogue is small enough that the section chips plus
         scrolling the list find anything faster than typing, and every row of chrome above the
         list pushed the two things the screen is for — the chips and the list — down.

         This page carried two searches in turn: a permanent Entry above the chips, then a magnifier
         button with a collapsible panel. Both are gone, along with the header row they lived in.
         The chip strip is now the primary way to narrow the list, and it is the first row, so it
         is the first thing under the tab bar; the product list starts higher than it ever has.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- Three rows: the chip strip, the product list, the cart. The list is the only `*` row,
         so it absorbs whatever the chips and the cart take. -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" Padding="16" RowSpacing="6">

        <!-- Single scrolling list of chips. It used to be a lone "Все" Button next to a
             CollectionView wrapped in a horizontal ScrollView, so the two scrollers competed
             for the same gesture.

             56, not 48. This was a real 2dp defect: the chip inside is 44dp tall with a 3dp
             margin all round, so 50dp of chip was being asked to fit a 48dp strip and every
             chip lost 2dp off its bottom. The strip is now exactly the chip plus its margins,
             with 6dp spare. The chip itself stays at 44dp because that is what the FilterChip
             style sets app-wide and 44dp is the height every chip in the app already measures at
             — the clipping was the defect, not the chip. -->
        <CollectionView ItemsSource="{Binding Categories}" SelectionMode="None" ItemTemplate="{StaticResource CategoryChipTemplate}" HeightRequest="56">
            <CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
                <LinearItemsLayout Orientation="Horizontal" ItemSpacing="6" />
            </CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
        </CollectionView>

        <CollectionView Grid.Row="1" ItemsSource="{Binding FilteredProducts}" SelectionMode="None" Margin="0,0,0,2">
            <CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
                <!-- The span is set from code (see MenuPage.PublishAvailableWidth), not bound.
                     Two reasons, both measured: ItemsLayout is a BindableObject outside the visual
                     tree, so a {Binding} on it does not reliably pick up the page's BindingContext;
                     and .NET MAUI 10 has no other way to state a column width — ItemWidth was
                     removed from ItemsLayout, and a WidthRequest or MaxWidthRequest on the card is
                     ignored for sizing because the platform's ItemsWrapGrid owns the container
                     width. Dropping to a single full-width column below the threshold is the one
                     value the grid still honours, and it cannot clip. -->
                <GridItemsLayout x:Name="ProductGridLayout" Orientation="Vertical" Span="2" HorizontalItemSpacing="4" VerticalItemSpacing="4" />
            </CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
            <CollectionView.EmptyView>
                <Label Text="Ничего не найдено." Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" />
            </CollectionView.EmptyView>
            <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="models:Product">
                    <!-- The card takes no WidthRequest, deliberately. MAUI 10 sizes the cell itself:
                         a literal WidthRequest="150" here still measured 190px columns, and a
                         MaxWidthRequest only shrank the content. The width contract is the grid's
                         Span, set from MenuPage.PublishAvailableWidth off
                         MenuViewModel.ProductColumnSpan — see the comment on the GridItemsLayout
                         above. Both columns come from the same layout, so they are always equal. -->
                    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Padding="6" StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 10">
                        <Border.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger TargetType="Border" Binding="{Binding IsAvailable}" Value="False">
                                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Border.Triggers>
                        <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                            <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.AddProductCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                        </Border.GestureRecognizers>
                        <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" RowSpacing="4">
                            <Label Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource RowTitle}" MaxLines="2" />
                            <Label Grid.Row="1" Text="{Binding ., Converter={StaticResource ProductPriceText}}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                            <Label Grid.Row="2" Text="Нет в наличии" Style="{StaticResource TagText}"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable, Converter={StaticResource InvertedBoolConverter}}" />
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>
                </DataTemplate>
            </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
        </CollectionView>

        <Border Grid.Row="2" Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Padding="6" Margin="0,0,0,0">
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="2">
                <Label Text="Корзина" FontSize="15" FontAttributes="Bold" />

                <!-- The cart is the one list that must stay scrollable. A fixed HeightRequest="180"
                     inside an Auto row squeezed the products list to a few cards on a short window.
                     MaximumHeightRequest bounds it proportionally: the cart takes what it needs
                     up to a cap, and the products list gets the rest.

                     MinimumHeightRequest is what makes the empty state exist at all. With only a
                     maximum, an Auto row collapses the CollectionView to zero when the cart is
                     empty, and the EmptyView had no room to render — measured on the emulator as
                     a 23px gap between "Корзина" and "Итого" with no "Пусто." anywhere, so the
                     empty cart looked identical to a cart that had failed to load. -->
                <CollectionView ItemsSource="{Binding Cart}" MinimumHeightRequest="44" MaximumHeightRequest="180" SelectionMode="None">
                    <CollectionView.EmptyView>
                        <VerticalStackLayout Padding="0,6">
                            <Label Text="Корзина пуста — выберите блюдо выше." Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" />
                        </VerticalStackLayout>
                    </CollectionView.EmptyView>
                    <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:CartItemViewModel">
                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6" Padding="0,2">
                                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                    <Label Text="{Binding ProductName}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="12" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />
                                    <Label Text="{Binding SelectedVariantName, StringFormat='[{0}]'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedVariantName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />
                                    <Label Text="{Binding SelectedModifierName, StringFormat='({0})'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedModifierName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />
                                </VerticalStackLayout>
                                <!-- The per-unit price was dropped from the row: it sat next to the
                                     bold line total and ate the width the stepper needed.
                                     Two 48dp buttons in solid Primary made the quantity look like
                                     a caption on its own controls rather than the value: measured
                                     on the emulator, 96dp of saturated fill flanked a 13sp digit in
                                     a 24dp column. The tap targets stay 48dp — that is the minimum
                                     every control here honours — and the weight moves instead, onto
                                     a 19sp bold number in a 40dp column with tonal buttons. -->
                                <Grid Grid.Column="1" ColumnDefinitions="48,40,48" ColumnSpacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                    <Button Text="−" Style="{StaticResource StepperButton}" SemanticProperties.Description="Убрать одну штуку" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.RemoveItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                    <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Quantity}" FontSize="19" FontAttributes="Bold" WidthRequest="40" HorizontalTextAlignment="Center" VerticalTextAlignment="Center" SemanticProperties.Description="Количество" />
                                    <Button Grid.Column="2" Text="+" Style="{StaticResource StepperButton}" SemanticProperties.Description="Добавить ещё одну" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.AddItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                </Grid>
                                <Label Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding LineTotal, StringFormat='{0:F2} ₽'}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="12" VerticalOptions="Center" HorizontalTextAlignment="End" />
                            </Grid>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                </CollectionView>

                <BoxView HeightRequest="1" Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray200}, Dark={StaticResource Gray600}}" />

                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" Padding="0,0,0,0">
                    <Label Text="Итого" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Total, StringFormat='{0:F2} ₽'}" FontSize="16" FontAttributes="Bold" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- The message sits above the button it reports on, not below it. "Заказ #1
                     создан на 640.00 ₽" is the most important feedback in the app and it was
                     the last line on the screen, in SecondaryLabel grey, styled identically to
                     a failure. Now it is bold, above the action, and coloured by outcome —
                     colour reinforcing the text, never replacing it.

                     The colour is a BINDING, and that is the whole point. It used to be two
                     DataTriggers setting TextColor to a bare {StaticResource Danger} and
                     {StaticResource Success}. A trigger's Setter takes a value, not a binding
                     expression, so each token was resolved once at parse time against the light
                     theme and could never follow a theme change: measured on a Pixel 7 in dark
                     theme this label painted Success #2E7D32 on #1E1E1E, 3.28:1, where
                     SuccessDark would have been 8.28:1. MenuViewModel.MessageColor resolves
                     against the live theme instead; see its remarks for the measurement. -->
                <Label Text="{Binding Message}" IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                       TextColor="{Binding MessageColor}" />

                <!-- Two first-class paths, side by side in one row so the cart does not grow
                     taller: "Оплатить и создать" opens the payment sheet and books the order
                     paid; "Создать без оплаты" defers payment. Both are obvious choices —
                     deferring payment is a normal part of a café's day (tabs, staff meals),
                     not a hidden default. -->
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                    <Button Grid.Column="0" Text="Оплатить и создать" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48" FontAttributes="Bold" Padding="0" IsEnabled="{Binding CanCreateOrder}" Command="{Binding PayAndCreateCommand}" />
                    <Button Grid.Column="1" Text="Создать без оплаты" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48" FontAttributes="Bold" Padding="0" IsEnabled="{Binding CanCreateOrder}" Command="{Binding CreateWithoutPaymentCommand}" />
                </Grid>
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>
